    {
        "content": "<p>I do have another question: Normally when talking about atomic propositions p and q, we implicitly assume that they are distinct. That way, we can define an assignment by saying \"Let A be the assignment sending p to True and q to False\".  Is there some way to encode that in Lean? I.e. I want something similar to <code>variable (p q : P)</code>, but with the assumption that they are distinct, and I want to be able to define a function <code>P -&gt; Bool</code> by defining in on the inputs p and q</p>",
        "id": 586363415,
        "sender_full_name": "RobQnadqs",
        "timestamp": 1776603818
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>One solution is to replace <code>variable [AtomType]</code> with <code>instance : AtomType where P := String</code> and use <code>\"p\"</code> and <code>\"q\"</code> instead of <code>(p q : P)</code>. Another solution is to include <code>p ≠ q</code> as a hypothesis.</p>",
        "id": 586366231,
        "sender_full_name": "Vlad",
        "timestamp": 1776605983
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I think for \"Let A be the assignment sending p to True and q to False\" you probably also want <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=DecidableEq#doc\">docs#DecidableEq</a> for the <code>P</code> in <code>AtomType</code>.</p>",
        "id": 586393459,
        "sender_full_name": "Malvin Gattinger",
        "timestamp": 1776629063
    }
]